{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789218623,"nonce":"O8hBeW9+g0B0EZamJgI8OzUV/as=","title":"Pool free read a large block's slab-masked address before its header","message":"A large block is allocated by the system allocator anywhere, so masking\nits address to the slab size points up to a slab's width before it,\nmemory the process may not have mapped. That read ran first, so freeing\na valid large block could fault. The 16 bytes ahead of the payload are\nalways the program's own memory for anything the pool handed out, so\nthat header is read first and the mask only once the block is known not\nto be large.","files":null}]}